WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.220
Hey this is Presh Talwalkar

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:10.440
A survey found that many adults could not answer math questions designed for 15 year old students.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.100
In this video i'm going to present a couple of the questions.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.740
Can you figure them out?

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.240
This question was missed by 25% of adults.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.020
There were some people on a train.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.080
19 people get off the train at the first stop.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.440
17 people get on the train.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.420
Now there are 63 people on the train.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.420
How many people were on the train to begin with?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.160
This question was missed by 46% of adults.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.680
An ordinary, fair dice is rolled 420 times.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.760
How many times is the number 3 expected?

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.000
Give these problems a try and when you're ready keep watching the video. I'm going to present the answers.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.920
For the train question, we'll consider the number of people who get off the train versus the number of people who get on.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:22.680
The 19 people who get off the train can be represented as -19 and the 17 people who get on can be represented as +17.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.080
-19 + 17 is equal to -2

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.580
This is interpreted as a net of 2 people got off the train.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.640
Originally the train had 2 more people than it ended with.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:45.020
Therefore the train began with 2 more than 63 which is 65 people.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:54.200
If a fair dice is rolled 420 times how many times is the number 3 expected?

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:06.440
In an ordinary fair dice, each of the 6 numbers is equally likely to occur so each number is expected to occur in one sixth of the total rolls.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:16.600
The number 3 is therefore expected to occur in one sixth of the 420 rolls or in 70 of the rolls.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.700
Did you figure these out?

